  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1124 PM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nCold frontal storms will be ongoing across areas south of I-44\nearly Thursday evening, with the storm axis continuing to push\nsouthward with the front. Dry conditions will likely arrive\nareawide by daybreak Friday. Much drier dew points and colder\ntemperatures will follow the front, along with a substantial\nincrease in northerly winds. Peak gusts Thursday night and into\nFriday will likely be in the 30 to 40 mph range. This does not\nappear to be a slam dunk Wind Advisory case but would not be\nsurprised to see a couple of observations with gusts above 40 mph.\nParts of far southeast Oklahoma that do not receive much rainfall\nfrom the current event may see a brief uptick in the potential for\nfire weather concerns on Friday given the gusty winds and much\nlower dew points that will be in place. \n\nSurface high pressure will move into the area Friday night and \ninto Saturday, in part, leading to the lowest overnight \ntemperatures of the season so far for both Friday night and \nSaturday night. Locations near the Kansas border will flirt with \nfreezing temperatures both mornings with both winds and humidities\nsupporting possible frost. Keep an eye on this potential in the \nnext couple of days if you have sensitive vegetation outdoors.\n\nSouth winds make a return Sunday, signaling the start of a warmup\nthat is expected to continue into at least the early to middle \npart of next week. Temperatures climb to near normal Monday \nafternoon and above Tuesday afternoon. The dry conditions that \nbegin Friday will extend through at least Wednesday, with low \nchances arriving late next week to western parts of the area due \nto another system approaching from the west.",
